==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|hōh}}, {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*hauhaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*kewk-}}, a suffixed form of {{m|ine-pro|*kew-}}. Compare Dutch {{m|nl|hoog}}, English {{m|en|high}}, Swedish {{m|sv|hög}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/hoːx/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-hoch.ogg|audio (Germany)|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-hoch.ogg|audio (Austria)|lang=de}}

===Adjective===
{{de-adj|höher|höchsten}}

# [[high]], [[tall]]
# [[large]], [[big]], [[heavy]]
# [[grand]], [[important]]
# {{lb|de|mathematics}} [[to the power of]]
#: ''fünf '''hoch''' vier''
#:: five to the power of four, (5⁴)

====Declension====
Since intervocalic /h/ became mute in the late Middle Ages, the adjective now has an irregular declension. The predicative form is ''hoch'' (Old High German spelling ''{{m|goh|hoh}}''), but declined forms use the stem ''hoh-'', e.g. ''der hohe Berg'' ("the high mountain"). The comparative and superlative degrees are {{m|de|höher}} and ''am'' {{m|de|höchsten}}, respectively.
{{de-decl-adj|hoh|höher|höchst}}

